config BK7258_MCUBOOT_IMAGE
	bool "Build CP application as an MCUboot payload"
	default n
	depends on ARCH_CHIP_BK7258 && !MCUBOOT_BOOTLOADER
	---help---
		Reserve the standard 0x200-byte MCUboot header before the CP or AP
		vector table.  The resulting NuttX binary is a payload, not a direct
		BL1 application: it must be signed by MCUboot imgtool and is entered
		through the board-owned BL2.  This changes neither the Beken SDK nor
		NuttX sources and is deliberately separate from the MCUboot BL2 profile.

config BK7258_BL2_IMAGE
	bool "Build the NuttX MCUboot BL2 for the dedicated RAM load address"
	default n
	depends on ARCH_CHIP_BK7258 && !BK7258_AP_CORE && MCUBOOT_BOOTLOADER
	select MTD_PARTITION
	---help---
		Build the NuttX MCUboot bootloader as the second boot stage, stored in
		the project-owned ``bl2`` flash partition but linked for execution at
		0x28020000.  The immutable first-stage bootloader copies it from the
		CRC-decoded XIP view into that RAM window before entering its vector
		table.  BL2 then owns the primary/secondary CP+AP application pair.
		The current BL2 image is kept within a conservative 64 KiB execution
		window.  If a larger logical BL2 is copied, its complete CRC-expanded
		XIP payload must be present; an erased CRC tail is invalid.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_BOOTSTRAP
	bool "N8-B1 AP SMP secondary bootstrap"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE
	select ARCH_HAVE_MULTICPU
	---help---
		Enable the first AP SMP bring-up boundary.  NuttX creates logical CPU1's
		IDLE TCB and calls up_cpu_start(); physical CPU2 enters the NuttX-aware
		secondary bootstrap, validates its vector/VTOR/MSP and allocated IDLE
		stack, publishes secondary_ready, then remains parked before scheduler
		online.  Ordinary tasks remain restricted to logical CPU0.

config BK7258_AP_IPI
	bool "N8-B2 AP bidirectional IPI wrapper"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_BOOTSTRAP
	select ARCH_IRQPRIO
	---help---
		Enable the AP-local logical CPU0 to logical CPU1 mailbox IPI wrapper.
		The wrapper uses the Beken SDK cross-core mailbox APIs while the
		team-owned SDK IRQ bridge routes source 63 through NuttX IRQ 79.  CPU2
		remains parked in WFI with scheduler online_mask 0x1; this option only
		proves bidirectional IRQ delivery and does not enable task scheduling on
		logical CPU1.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_SCHED_ONLINE
	bool "N8-C1 AP secondary scheduler-online idle"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_IPI && SMP
	---help---
		Move physical CPU2 / AP logical CPU1 from the N8-B2 private WFI
		park loop into the NuttX nx_idle_trampoline path.  Scheduler and SMP
		call IPIs continue through the team wrapper and Beken SDK cross-core
		mailbox path; no direct MBOX0 data plane is added.  The first gate keeps
		CONFIG_SMP_DEFAULT_CPUSET=0x1, so ordinary tasks remain on logical CPU0.
		Graceful CPU hot-unplug is not provided; stop/restart/cycle are rejected
		while this scheduler-online mode is active.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_AFFINITY
	bool "N8-C2 AP explicit CPU1 affinity one-task gate"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_SCHED_ONLINE && !DISABLE_PTHREAD
	---help---
		Create exactly one detached diagnostic pthread whose initial affinity
		mask is logical CPU1 only (0x2).  The task must remote-dispatch through
		the existing scheduler IPI wrapper, wake CPU1 from IDLE, run its body
		once and release its PID before AP READY is published.
		CONFIG_SMP_DEFAULT_CPUSET remains
		0x1; this option does not enable default CPU1 placement, free migration,
		load balancing or a second CPU1 task.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_SEM_WAKE
	bool "N8-C3 AP CPU1 semaphore block/wake gate"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_AFFINITY
	---help---
		Extend the single N8-C2 CPU1-affinity pthread so it blocks on one
		NuttX semaphore.  Logical CPU0 proves the exact task is in
		TSTATE_WAIT_SEM on that semaphore, posts exactly once, and requires the
		existing scheduler IPI path to wake the task on logical CPU1.
		CONFIG_SMP_DEFAULT_CPUSET remains 0x1; no second diagnostic task is
		created and ordinary tasks remain restricted to logical CPU0.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_SEM_WAKE_LOOP
	bool "N8-C4 AP CPU1 fixed semaphore wake loop gate"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_SEM_WAKE
	---help---
		Reuse the single detached N8-C3 CPU1-affinity pthread and semaphore for
		exactly eight ordered block/wake cycles.  The first cycle preserves the
		N8-C3 BSEM proof while the separate BSWL record proves all eight wakes.
		CONFIG_SMP_DEFAULT_CPUSET remains 0x1; no second task, semaphore or
		runtime-configurable cycle count is added.

choice
	prompt "N8-C5..D1 advanced SMP gate (one at a time)"
	default BK7258_AP_SMP_ADVANCED_NONE
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_SEM_WAKE_LOOP
	---help---
		Select exactly one N8-C5..D1 advanced SMP diagnostic gate.
		All options share the generic 32-word advanced-stage ABI struct.
		CONFIG_SMP_DEFAULT_CPUSET remains 0x1.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_ADVANCED_NONE
	bool "None (N8-C4 baseline only)"
	---help---
		No advanced gate.  Only N8-C4 behavior is active.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_BIDIR_PINGPONG
	bool "N8-C5 bidirectional semaphore pingpong"
	---help---
		Two detached tasks: initiator on CPU0 (mask 0x1) and responder on
		CPU1 (mask 0x2).  Two zero-count static semaphores.  Fixed 8 round
		trips.  Each poster must prove exact counterpart waiter before post.
		Expected: CPU0->CPU1 +9, CPU1->CPU0 +8, calls +17.  Failures
		coalesced/stale/spurious zero.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_DUALTASK
	bool "N8-C6 dual CPU1 local scheduling"
	---help---
		Two detached tasks both mask 0x2 and two zero-count semaphores.
		Both first block; CPU0 posts task A once.  Fixed 8 A and 8 B
		executions; A posts B each round, B posts A rounds 1..7.
		Expected: CPU0->CPU1 +3, CPU1->CPU0 +0, calls +3.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CONTROLLED_MIGRATION
	bool "N8-C7 controlled migration"
	---help---
		One detached task created with mask 0x1.  Exactly 8 self-affinity
		transitions using pthread_setaffinity_np: odd->0x2/CPU1,
		even->0x1/CPU0.  Expected: tx/rx +4 each direction, calls +8.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_CPU1_TIMED_WAKE
	bool "N8-C8 timed wake"
	---help---
		One detached task created with mask 0x2.  Exactly 8 nxsig_usleep
		(20000us) cycles, returning on CPU1 each time.  Expected:
		CPU0->CPU1 +8, CPU1->CPU0 +0, calls +8.

config BK7258_AP_SMP_LIFECYCLE_QUIESCE
	bool "N8-D1 CPU1 scheduler quiesce/resume foundation"
	---help---
		Dispatch one asynchronous SMP callback to CPU1, hold that callback in
		a bounded WFE loop, and resume it from CPU0 with a shared sequence plus
		SEV.  CPU1 remains scheduler-online throughout.  The scheduler-online
		secondary-stop API must remain fail-closed with -ENOTSUP.  This is not
		CPU hot-unplug and does not reset or power down physical CPU2.
		Expected: CPU0->CPU1 +1, CPU1->CPU0 +0, calls +1.

endchoice

config BK7258_AP_CONTROL
	bool "CPU0 control for the CPU1 AP NuttX image"
	default n
	depends on ARCH_CHIP_BK7258 && !BK7258_AP_CORE && SYSTEM_NSH
	select BUILTIN
	select NSH_BUILTIN_APPS
	---help---
		Add the CPU0 start/stop/restart wrapper and the manual apctl NSH
		command.  The baseline protocol uses a reserved shared SRAM page;
		BK7258_RPTUN_MBOX replaces the raw doorbell path with the SDK mailbox
		channel wrapper when the RPTUN profile is selected.

config BK7258_AP_AUTOSTART
	bool "Start CPU1 AP during board bring-up"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_CONTROL
	---help---
		Automatically start CPU1 after CPU0 board services initialize.
		Keep this disabled until the dual image has been flashed and the
		manual apctl start/status path is board-verified.

config BK7258_AP_AUTOSTART_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "CPU1 AP autostart timeout (milliseconds)"
	default 30000 if BK7258_RPTUN
	default 15000
	range 1000 60000
	depends on BK7258_AP_AUTOSTART
	---help---
		Bound the board bring-up wait for AP READY.  RPTUN profiles run the
		preserved N8 SMP gates before registering the AP RPTUN endpoint, so
		they need a longer cold-reset deadline than the non-RPTUN baseline.

config BK7258_RPTUN_LAYOUT
	bool "N9 CP/AP RPTUN shared-memory layout"
	default n
	depends on ARCH_CHIP_BK7258
	---help---
		Reserve 0x28097000..0x2809efff for the CP/AP RPTUN control,
		resource table, vrings and RPMsg buffers.  On the AP image this
		shrinks the linked RAM window and moves the physical CPU2 boot stack
		down by 32 KiB.  This option is layout-only: it does not enable the
		NuttX RPTUN/OpenAMP transport or change the N7/N8 baseline profiles.

config BK7258_RPTUN_MBOX
	bool "N9 unified CP/AP MBOX0 transport"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN_LAYOUT
	depends on !BK7258_AP_CORE || BK7258_AP_IPI
	select ARCH_IRQPRIO
	select BK7258_SDK_IRQ_BRIDGE if !BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Use the Beken SDK MBOX0 FIFO as the single owner for AP-local SMP
		commands and CP/AP lifecycle/RPTUN notifications.  Raw data[1] == 0
		remains reserved for AP SMP; CP/AP traffic uses the SDK adapter's
		16-byte message format.  The IRQ callback only coalesces messages and
		wakes a pinned bk7258-rptun-rx kthread.

config BK7258_RPTUN
	bool "N9 CP/AP NuttX RPTUN and RPMsg link"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN_MBOX
	select DEV_SIMPLE_ADDRENV
	select RPTUN
	select RPMSG_PING
	---help---
		Instantiate one CP-master/AP-remote RPTUN link over the reserved
		shared SRAM and the SDK mailbox-channel wrapper.  CP is the virtio
		driver, AP is the virtio device, and the built-in RPMsg ping endpoint
		is enabled for the N9 functional and performance gates.

config BK7258_PM_CLOCK
	bool "CP-owned peripheral clock voting service"
	default y if BK7258_RPTUN
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN
	select BK7258_PM_POLICY if !BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Keep BK7258 peripheral leaf-clock ownership on CP and expose a bounded,
		generation-safe RPMsg get/put wrapper to AP drivers.  The server maps a
		project-owned allow-list onto the official v3.1.1.9 sys_ctrl API and
		reference-counts each clock.  This is the clock-ownership foundation;
		The CP image also selects the board PM policy below.

config BK7258_TEMPERATURE
	bool "CP-owned on-die temperature sensor"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN
	select SCHED_LPWORK
	select THERMAL if BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Use the immutable CP SDK's bounded one-shot SARADC measurement and
		expose it to AP through a project-owned, generation-safe RPMsg endpoint.
		The SDK result is an ADC raw code, not degrees Celsius.  AP publishes a
		NuttX thermal zone only after a valid per-device 25 C reference has been
		supplied; uncalibrated reads retain the raw code without inventing an
		absolute temperature.  CP sampling runs on LPWORK so the SDK's retry
		deadline cannot block the RPTUN receive worker.

config BK7258_TEMPERATURE_FIXED_REFERENCE_RAW
	int "Fixed 25 C temperature reference raw code"
	default 0
	range 0 1364
	depends on BK7258_TEMPERATURE && BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Set this only for a specific calibrated device or a bounded laboratory
		image.  Zero leaves Celsius conversion disabled until an application
		loads that chip's factory 25 C raw code with the runtime API.  A nonzero
		value must be in the SDK-valid 11..1364 interval; the runtime rejects the
		Kconfig-only 1..10 gap.  This is per-device calibration and must not be
		treated as a board-wide nominal.

config BK7258_TEMPERATURE_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Thermal-zone polling interval (milliseconds)"
	default 1000
	range 100 60000
	depends on BK7258_TEMPERATURE && BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Polling interval used after calibrated data enables the NuttX thermal
		zone.  Raw reads made directly through the board API are on demand.

config BK7258_TEMPERATURE_VALIDATION
	bool "Run bounded on-die temperature validation"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_TEMPERATURE && BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Perform eight AP-to-CP reads after normal peripheral bring-up and place
		the raw range, generation and final status in a debugger-visible record.
		This validates the real RPMsg and SARADC path without assuming an
		absolute Celsius value when factory calibration is unavailable.

config BK7258_PM_POLICY
	bool "CP-owned NuttX PM and multi-client DVFS policy"
	default y
	depends on BK7258_PM_CLOCK && !BK7258_AP_CORE
	select PM
	select PM_GOVERNOR_GREEDY
	select BK7258_DVFS
	---help---
		Initialize the stock NuttX PM framework during ARM bring-up and keep
		all BK7258 frequency decisions on CP.  SDK module votes and AP RPMsg
		votes are translated to stable board client IDs and the highest active
		vote wins, matching v3.1.1.9.  The official 120 MHz default vote is the
		floor.  The board idle loop integrates NuttX PM with ordinary Cortex-M
		WFI.  Without BK7258_PM_COORDINATED_STANDBY this admits PM_IDLE only;
		PM_STANDBY and PM_SLEEP remain fail-closed.

config BK7258_PM_FAULT_INJECTION
	bool "PM RPMsg reply-loss fault injection"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_PM_CLOCK && !BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Expose a CP diagnostic record whose drop_reply_count field can be
		armed by a debugger or initialized by the validation-only option below.
		The PM server then commits a request but intentionally suppresses its
		response, allowing a real board to verify AP retry and
		generation/sequence replay without changing the
		request or applying its resource transition twice.  The hook is inert
		until explicitly armed and must remain disabled in production profiles.

config BK7258_PM_FAULT_INITIAL_DROP_COUNT
	int "Initial PM reply drop count"
	default 0
	range 0 3
	depends on BK7258_PM_FAULT_INJECTION
	---help---
		Initialize the validation record before the CP PM endpoint is exposed.
		A value of three suppresses the original reply and both AP retries, so
		the following cleanup operation must recover the cached transaction
		before it can release the resource.  Keep this at zero except for a
		bounded fault-injection image; production profiles do not select the
		parent option at all.

config BK7258_PM_COORDINATED_STANDBY
	bool "CP/AP coordinated low-voltage standby"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN && BK7258_RPTUN_MBOX && BK7258_PM_CLOCK
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE || TIMER_ARCH
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE || !SCHED_TICKLESS
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE || !ARM_SYSTICK_IRQ_WQUEUE
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE || BK7258_PM_POLICY
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE || BK7258_AP_CONTROL
	depends on !BK7258_AP_CORE || SMP
	depends on !BK7258_AP_CORE || BK7258_AP_IPI
	depends on !BK7258_AP_CORE || BK7258_AP_SMP_SCHED_ONLINE
	select PM if BK7258_AP_CORE
	select PM_GOVERNOR_GREEDY if BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Implement the v3.1.1.9 low-voltage contract across CP, both AP logical
		CPUs, AON r3 and the SDK-owned mailbox.  AP idle cores stop SysTick,
		check NVIC/SYS/DMA pending work, preserve only mailbox wake, publish
		the official WFI bits and restore their complete interrupt state.  CP
		enters low voltage only after both AP votes, all AP clock/sleep votes
		and the RPTUN data plane are idle, with an AON RTC wake source armed.
		The CP restore path repairs flash, watchdog and SysTick, then queues a
		hard-IRQ SysTick trampoline that advances the NuttX architecture timer
		from AON elapsed time before ordinary scheduling resumes.  PM_SLEEP
		remains rejected: this option enables only the resumable PM_STANDBY
		state.  Do not enable it in RTT profiles, which deliberately keep the CP
		awake for an unattached debugger.

config BK7258_PM_STANDBY_ONESHOT_VERIFY
	bool "Stop after one coordinated standby wake for hardware verification"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_PM_COORDINATED_STANDBY && !BK7258_AP_CORE
	depends on !BK7258_CONSOLE_RTT
	---help---
		Allow exactly one successful CP low-voltage entry and wake, then reject
		later PM_STANDBY transitions so SWD can reconnect while the system uses
		ordinary PM_IDLE.  This is a bounded hardware-verification aid for
		reading the CP/AP diagnostic records after the low-voltage domain has
		powered the debug access port down.  Leave it disabled in production;
		it does not alter the coordinated standby protocol itself.

config BK7258_RPMSG_TEST
	bool "N9 AP-SMP RPMsg bounded concurrency test"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_RPTUN && !DISABLE_PTHREAD
	depends on !BK7258_AP_CORE || SMP
	select BUILTIN if !BK7258_AP_CORE
	select NSH_BUILTIN_APPS if !BK7258_AP_CORE
	---help---
		Register a board-local RPMsg endpoint on both images.  The CP-side
		bkrpmsgtest command asks two AP pthreads, pinned to logical CPU0 and
		CPU1, to send concurrent echo traffic with bounded non-blocking sends
		and bounded waits.  The result records sender CPU, AP callback CPU,
		generation, loss and cycle-count latency percentiles using machine-
		parsable BRPT lines.  The RPTUN RX worker remains the only transport
		owner and keeps its higher real-time priority.

