	select BK7258_PSRAM_MEDIA
	help
		Configure the GC2145/DVP path for 640x480 packed YUYV frames instead
		of MJPEG.  /dev/video0 remains a standard NuttX V4L2 capture device.
		Select this when a board media owner needs raw pixels for a hardware
		encoder or another image-processing stage.

config BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_H264
	bool "Expose the T5-Board camera as live H.264"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA && !BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_RAW_YUV
	depends on BK7258_AP_SMP_SCHED_ONLINE
	help
		Configure the GC2145 and the official v3.1.1.9 DVP/YUV/H.264
		pipeline as one live stream.  /dev/video0 advertises the standard
		V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264 format and returns Annex-B access units.  The SDK
		routes the H.264 interrupt to the second AP hardware core, so that
		logical CPU must be online.

		The pinned NuttX version publishes the H.264 V4L2 fourcc but omits an
		internal imgdata H.264 token.  The BK7258 lower half contains a narrow
		compatibility mapping for this configured instance; no NuttX source or
		public ABI is changed.

config BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_VALIDATION
	bool "Capture one MJPEG frame during T5-Board camera bring-up"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA && !BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_RAW_YUV
	depends on !BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_H264
	help
		Run one bounded V4L2 USERPTR capture after /dev/video0 is
		registered.  The validation uses a PSRAM buffer, waits at most three
		seconds for one 640x480 MJPEG frame, checks its JPEG markers, reports
		BKCAM PASS/FAIL through syslog, and then closes the stream.  Enable
		this only in drivercheck profiles; normal board configurations merely
		register the standard V4L2 device.

config BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_H264_VALIDATION
	bool "Capture one live H.264 frame during T5-Board bring-up"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_T5_BOARD_CAMERA_H264
	help
		Run one bounded V4L2 USERPTR capture after /dev/video0 is registered.
		It keeps the GC2145/DVP/YUV/H.264 pipeline live, validates a non-empty
		640x480 Annex-B frame, reports BKCAMH264 PASS/FAIL, and closes the
		stream.  Enable this only in drivercheck profiles.

config BK7258_USBHOST
	bool "BK7258 native USB host controller"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE && BK7258_BOARD_T5_BOARD
	select SCHED_HPWORK
	select USBHOST
	select USBHOST_HAVE_ASYNCH
	select USBHOST_WAITER
	help
		Expose the BK7258 MUSB controller through NuttX's standard USB host
		controller interface.  NuttX owns enumeration and class drivers; the
		immutable v3.1.1.9 SDK supplies only power/PHY setup and the low-level
		HCD pipe/URB implementation.  This option is limited to T5-Board,
		whose USB-A connector is wired to the native host D+/D- pins and 5 V.

config BK7258_USBHOST_CH34X
	bool "CH340/CH341 USB host serial class"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_USBHOST && !USBHOST_BULK_DISABLE
	select ARCH_HAVE_SERIAL_TERMIOS
	select SCHED_LPWORK
	select SERIAL
	select SERIAL_REMOVABLE
	select SERIAL_TERMIOS
	select USBHOST_ASYNCH
	help
		Register a board-owned NuttX USB host class for Winchiphead
		VID 0x1a86/PID 0x7523 and publish the connected converter as
		/dev/ttyUSB0.  The first implementation is deliberately limited to
		115200 8N1 with no flow control.

config BK7258_USBHOST_CH34X_VALIDATION
	bool "Run CH34x TX/RX loopback validation"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_USBHOST_CH34X
	help
		Open /dev/ttyUSB0 after enumeration, transmit a fixed byte pattern,
		and report whether it is received back.  Short TXD to RXD on the USB
		serial adapter before enabling this drivercheck-only option.

config BK7258_RTC
	bool "AON RTC lower half"
	default n
	select BK7258_SDK_IPC_RUNTIME
	select RTC
	select RTC_DATETIME
	select RTC_DRIVER

config BK7258_SARADC
	bool "BK7258 SAR ADC lower half"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AP_CORE
	select BK7258_SDK_IPC_RUNTIME
	select ANALOG
	select ADC
	help
		Expose one AP-side SARADC channel through the standard NuttX ADC
		upper half.  The lower half maps the configured analog channel while
		/dev/adcN is open, but acquires the CP-owned controller only for each
		ANIOC_TRIGGER conversion.  This keeps the on-die temperature path and
		other SARADC users live between samples.

		The matching CP image must enable BK7258_SARADC_SERVER because the
		v3.1.1.9 AP client uses the SDK mailbox service rather than touching
		SARADC locally.  The selected board remains responsible for assigning
		the channel's physical pin exclusively and rejecting conflicting GPIO,
		UART, USB, touch, or other pin owners.

config BK7258_SARADC_CHAN
	int "SAR ADC channel"
	default 0
	range 0 15
	depends on BK7258_SARADC

config BK7258_SARADC_BUS
	int "SAR ADC device minor"
	default 0
	range 0 15
	depends on BK7258_SARADC

config BK7258_SARADC_READ_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "SAR ADC conversion timeout (ms)"
	default 1000
	range 1 2000
	depends on BK7258_SARADC
	help
		Bound the CP-side wait for the SDK's 32-sample conversion buffer.  The
		upper bound deliberately remains below the immutable AP mailbox
		operation timeout so the response and completion handshake retain
		time to finish.

config BK7258_SARADC_VALIDATION
	bool
	default n
	depends on BK7258_SARADC && BK7258_AP_CORE
	help
		Build the chip-generic, bounded /dev/adcN lifecycle and transition
		validator.  A selected-board binding supplies the expected channel,
		active direction and relative thresholds; this implementation contains
		no physical GPIO, key, battery or voltage-divider assumptions.

config BK7258_SDIO
	bool
	default n
	depends on BK7258_PM_CLOCK
	select BK7258_SDK_IPC_RUNTIME
	select ARCH_HAVE_SDIO
	select SCHED_HPWORK
	help
		Expose the BK7258 SDK blocking SDIO host controller as a NuttX
		sdio_dev_s lower half.  Physical pin mux, slot presence policy and
		bus-width prerequisites belong to the selected board binding, which
		selects this hidden controller capability.  HPWORK remains required by
		NuttX's unconditional SDIO callback-registration ABI.

